$(2)$ To find the value of this expression, we'll first use the given trigonometric identity $\tan \alpha = \frac{\sin \alpha}{\cos \alpha}$ and the Pythagorean identity $\sin^{2}\alpha + \cos^{2}\alpha = 1$ to simplify the expression.
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{1}{4}\sin^{2}\alpha + \frac{1}{3}\sin \alpha \cos \alpha + \frac{1}{2}\cos^{2}\alpha &= \frac{\frac{1}{4}\sin^{2}\alpha + \frac{1}{3}\sin \alpha \cos \alpha + \frac{1}{2}\cos^{2}\alpha}{\sin^{2}\alpha + \cos^{2}\alpha} \\
&= \frac{\frac{1}{4}\tan^{2}\alpha + \frac{1}{3}\tan \alpha + \frac{1}{2}}{\tan^{2}\alpha + 1} \\
&= \frac{\frac{1}{4}(2)^{2} + \frac{1}{3}(2) + \frac{1}{2}}{(2)^{2} + 1} \\
&= \boxed{\frac{13}{30}}
\end{aligned}
$$
